        { type: "multipleChoice", question: "The Counseling Compact operates through which authorization model?", options: ["License portability — your home license is valid everywhere", "Privilege-to-practice — authorization linked to home state license", "Universal licensure — one national license for all states", "Reciprocity — automatic recognition of equivalent licenses"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "The Compact creates a privilege-to-practice model. A counselor with an active, unencumbered license in their home state may apply for a privilege to practice in other member states — not a separate license, but an authorization dependent on the home state license." },
        { type: "multipleChoice", question: "As of early 2025, approximately how many states have enacted or are considering PSYPACT (the psychology interstate compact)?", options: ["10-15 states", "20-25 states", "40+ states and territories", "All 50 states"], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "PSYPACT has been enacted by over 40 states and territories, making it the most established behavioral health interstate compact. Georgia has enacted PSYPACT." }
